# Integral Core Rules

## Project identity

- **Integral** — local-first desktop app for holistic life tracking (ratings, checklists, metrics, notes, guidance, fitness programs).
- **Tagline:** *Tend every area of your life. One honest day at a time.*
- **Repo:** https://github.com/earthboundtrev/integral
- Root **`README.md`** is the public product face (tagline, features, download, roadmap summary). Do not rewrite it casually. Put **new technical docs** under `docs/`.

## Product principles

- **Local-first** — no mandatory cloud account; user data stays on machine (`data/data.json` in dev, `%APPDATA%\Integral\` when frozen).
- **Low friction** — rating + Save is enough on low-energy days; everything else optional.
- **Intelligent maintenance** — guidance engine should surface trends and gaps, not just store logs.
- **Official fitness tables** — program progressions live in `programs/*.json`; do not invent rep standards.

## Scope and quality

- **Minimal diffs** — change only what the task requires; match existing naming and patterns.
- After Python changes: `python -m py_compile` on touched modules; prefer `python -m pytest` (targeted) over full suite when iterating.
- Prefer focused modules over growing `personal_dev_tracker.py` without reason.
- **Ticket workflow:** pointer pipeline `IMPLEMENT → BUGBOT → TESTS → ARCHITECT → DEPLOY` until human sign-off — `.cursor/rules/ticket-lifecycle-loop.mdc`. Any code fix rewinds to **BUGBOT**.

## Model strategy (ticket work)

Human approval happens at **issue/PR intent** (and approved specs) — not via a pre-code architect gate. Use **different agents/models per role** (`.cursor/rules/ticket-lifecycle-loop.mdc`). **Cost default (aligned with Ducking Gravity / SmartAstro 8/18/2026):** implement on Composer Fast; Bugbot/Architect/security on **Grok 4.6 High** — do **not** omit `model` on checks (costly default) and do **not** burn Opus/Sonnet on routine reviews.

| Phase | Stage | Who / what | Primary → fallback(s) |
|-------|-------|------------|------------------------|
| **Implement** | IMPLEMENT | Parent / implement Task | `composer-2.5-fast` → `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast` |
| **Bugbot** | BUGBOT | Separate **`bugbot`** Task — never self-review | `cursor-grok-4.6-high-fast` → `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast` → omit |
| **Tests** | TESTS | Same session | — |
| **Architect** | ARCHITECT | Separate Architect Task (`review-architect`) | `cursor-grok-4.6-high-fast` → `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast` → omit |
| **Deploy** | DEPLOY | Default Agent | Composer → Grok / parent |
| **Security** | (within BUGBOT→DEPLOY) | `security-review` when risky | `cursor-grok-4.6-high-fast` → `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast` → omit |

Outages: `.cursor/rules/subagent-watchdog.mdc`. Prefer local Tasks: `.cursor/rules/prefer-local-agents.mdc`. **New ticket:** `.cursor/rules/github-new-ticket.mdc`.

**Architect blockers → rewind pointer to BUGBOT**, then forward through TESTS and ARCHITECT again. Full rules: `.cursor/rules/ticket-lifecycle-loop.mdc`.

Do **not** run the heavy architect review before implementation unless the user explicitly asks for upfront design on a large/cross-cutting change.

## Git workflow (GitHub)

**Before writing code** for a GitHub issue or feature branch, sync with `main`.

### 1. Load issue context

- Run `gh issue view {N}` (or read the issue) for acceptance criteria.
- Note the issue number for commits and PR description (`Closes #N`).
- If a feature needs a new approved spec, finish ADR-007 / spec approval before IMPLEMENT.

### 2. Update local `main`

```powershell
git fetch origin
git checkout main
git pull origin main
```

### 3. Branch — new or existing

**New work (default):**

```powershell
git checkout -b feature/short-description-{N}
```

**Continuing existing branch:** sync with `main` before more commits:

```powershell
git checkout feature/your-existing-branch
git merge origin/main
```

Use **merge** (not rebase) unless the user explicitly requests rebase. Resolve conflicts now — do not defer to PR time.

### 4. If merge conflicts occur

1. Run `git status` — list conflicted files.
2. Resolve markers intentionally (do not blindly pick one side).
3. Run `python -m py_compile` on touched modules and relevant tests.
4. `git add` resolved files → `git commit` (merge commit is fine).

### 5. During implementation

- Implement against the issue acceptance criteria on the feature branch only — never commit directly to `main` unless the user explicitly asks.
- Keep commits focused; do not open the PR until §5a and §5b are complete.
- **Never** commit `data/`, secrets, or API keys.

### 5a. BUGBOT & TESTS stages

Pipeline: `.cursor/rules/ticket-lifecycle-loop.mdc`. Pointer must reach **TESTS** only after **BUGBOT** is clean on the current diff.

#### BUGBOT (repeat until clean)

- Launch **Bugbot** per Cursor `review-bugbot` skill (`Diff: branch changes`).
- **Fix valid findings** → stay at **BUGBOT**, re-run on updated diff.
- Do not advance to **TESTS** or **ARCHITECT** while substantive Bugbot findings remain.

#### TESTS (after Bugbot clean)

- Map ACs to tests under `tests/`; name regression tests with the issue number when practical (`test_*_45.py` or clear issue refs in the test module docstring).
- Run `python -m py_compile` on touched modules and `python -m pytest` for the relevant suites (add `tests/test_backup.py` / `tests/test_integral_io.py` when persistence changed).
- Failures or material test-driven code changes → rewind pointer to **BUGBOT**.

### 5b. ARCHITECT stage

**After BUGBOT clean and TESTS pass.** Architect blockers fixed → **rewind to BUGBOT** (not ARCHITECT-only), then forward through TESTS → ARCHITECT again.

Run **`review-architect`** (`/review-architect`) or an explicit pass per **`.cursor/rules/integral-architect.mdc`**. Launch as a **separate** Task with **`model: "cursor-grok-4.6-high-fast"`** (never implementer self-certify; never omit `model` on checks).

Check against:

- `docs/architecture.md` and `.cursor/rules/architecture.mdc`
- Acceptance criteria fully met; no scope creep
- Export/import continuity when data touched (`.cursor/rules/backup-export.mdc`)
- Docs/README Features if user-facing (`.cursor/rules/documentation.mdc`) — see §5b.1
- Obvious tech debt, duplication, or wrong-layer code

**Fix substantive findings** before opening the PR. For encryption, vault, sync, AI, or schema migration: also run **security-review** per `.cursor/rules/security-review.mdc`.

#### 5b.1 README Features audit

During the architect pass (not a separate agent step). Compare shipped behavior to root `README.md` **Features** / **What you get**.

| Do | Don't |
|----|--------|
| Add a concise bullet/row when the ticket introduces **new product behavior** a user would name as a feature | Rewrite tagline, license, or other portfolio prose casually |
| Keep install/download accurate | Put implementation detail in README — that belongs in `docs/` |
| Include README edits in the same PR as the feature | Update README for every bug fix or internal refactor |

**Examples that need a README mention:** full zip backup with creative docs, soft Overview refresh, a new Fitness Hub surface. **Examples that skip:** typo fix, pure performance internal cache with no UX copy change already covered, test-only work.

### 5c. DEPLOY stage

**Only after ARCHITECT merge-ready.**

1. Proportionate validate (`pytest` targeted + `py_compile`; full suite when risky).
2. Push; open/update PR with **`Closes #N`** in the body (required — title alone does not close the issue).
3. Watch CI / PR comments — code changes → **IMPLEMENT**, then **BUGBOT** before skipping ahead.
4. Merge/close **only when the human explicitly asks**.
5. When merging: `gh pr merge {PR} --merge --delete-branch`, then run `.\scripts\post_merge_hygiene.ps1 -Issue {N} -PR {PR}` (or manually **verify** `gh issue view {N}` is `CLOSED` and close it if not). Close any duplicate open issues for the same work. Delete the local feature branch.
6. Until merge/sign-off **and** the issue is closed, keep reporting pipeline pointer; do not mark ticket done.

### 6. Confirm before coding

```powershell
git branch --show-current
git log --oneline -1 origin/main
git status -sb
```

Working tree should be clean (or only intentional WIP). Branch should include latest `main` before new changes.

## Documentation

- **New technical docs** → `docs/` (not new root `.md` files unless README capability bullet).
- **Architecture** → `docs/architecture.md`
- **Roadmap** → `docs/ROADMAP.md` — update when shipping roadmap-tier features
- **User-facing features** → update root `README.md` **Features** section in the same PR (`.cursor/rules/documentation.mdc`)
- **Releases** → build with `.\build.ps1`; attach zip to GitHub Releases

## Build & release

- Dev: `.\run.ps1`
- Exe: `.\scripts\build_exe.ps1` → `dist\Integral\Integral.exe`
- Icon: `scripts/build_icon.py` from `assets/integral-icon-source.png`
- See `.cursor/rules/build-release.mdc` for PyInstaller constraints

### Release hygiene (required when shipping a version)

On every release push to `main`, keep **only the latest** artifact and tag:

1. `git rm` older `Integral-v*-windows.zip` files; add only the new zip matching `APP_VERSION` in `paths.py`
2. Tag `v{VERSION}` on the release commit; delete all older `v*` tags locally and on `origin`
3. Push `main` and the new tag; `git push origin --delete` each stale remote tag
4. **Validate README** — compare CHANGELOG / commits since last tag; if any user-facing feature shipped, `README.md` Features (“What you get”) must already mention it before the release commit is pushed. See `.cursor/rules/build-release.mdc` → **README Features gate**.

Full checklist and PowerShell examples: `.cursor/rules/build-release.mdc` → **Release hygiene**.

